in this tiny makeshift kitchen on the  outskirts of lima women put together  whatever they can to create a nutritious  meal on the menu recently was rice  carrots corn and chicken bones cut up in  tiny pieces so everyone can get some  protein says reina franco  we only eat lunch there is no budget for  breakfast and in the evenings each one  has to figure out what to put on their  tables communal kitchens around the  country were her response to the  pandemic that left 4 million  impoverished people without jobs more  than 250 000 feet on meals that cost  less than 30 cents  rising fuel prices have made some  ingredients like meat or chicken  impossible to buy and local government's  donations to keep them going are waning  in some areas government distribution of  basic ingredients stopped more than four  months ago  the local government does not remember  us at the top of the hill they offer  help during political campaigns but then  they forget about us  some of these communal kitchens feed 20  to 30 people every day others two to  three hundred many say this is the last  resort to feed their families and they  say if they didn't have this they  wouldn't know where to turn to for help  to alleviate the pressure the government  has suspended some taxes on fuel and  some basic products but analysts say  that's insufficient  no doubt there is hunger and it's going  to get worse inflation is here to stay  for a long time reducing the purchasing  power of households particularly of the  poorest the government apparently  believes that prices can be controlled  but this is not possible  the finance ministry already estimates  this year investment growth will be zero  community leader edith accurio blames  president pedro castillo  the president's priority is not the  vulnerable families i feel angry because  he should be concerned about the people  in need  leaders of the near 3000 kitchens around  the country say they are desperate the  women have postponed a nationwide  protest waiting for the government to  resolve the food crisis but they say  their children are hungry and time is  running out mariana sanchez lima peru
 

